---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import DocsVisual from "../../components/docs/DocsVisual.astro";
import WorktreeMock from "../../components/mocks/WorktreeMock.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "where", title: "Checkout or worktree" },
  { id: "choose", title: "Choose for each thread" },
  { id: "worktrees", title: "How a worktree is made" },
  { id: "move", title: "Move a thread" },
  { id: "merge", title: "Bring the work back" },
  { id: "delete", title: "Delete a thread" },
  { id: "repository", title: "What OpenOrc adds to your repository" },
  { id: "no-git", title: "Projects without Git" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout slug="git" sections={sections}>
  <DocsVisual
    art="feature-worktree"
    label="OpenOrc with the thread Simplify the design tokens open. It works in its own worktree, and the row under the composer names its branch, openorc/design-tokens-2c91f0, with 12 lines added, 4 removed, and Commit changes. In the sidebar, another thread in the project is still working."
  >
    <WorktreeMock />
  </DocsVisual>

  <section aria-labelledby="where">
    <h2 id="where">Checkout or worktree</h2>
    <p>Each thread in a project works in one of two places.</p>
    <dl class="docs-terms">
      <div>
        <dt>Local checkout</dt>
        <dd>Your repository folder, on whatever branch you have checked out. The agent edits the same files you do, and so does every other thread on the checkout.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Worktree</dt>
        <dd>A separate folder with a branch of its own, linked to the same repository. The thread's edits stay out of your checkout until you bring them over.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>Use the checkout for small changes you want to run and try right away. Use a worktree when several threads work on one project at once, or when you want the work kept apart until you have reviewed it.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="choose">
    <h2 id="choose">Choose for each thread</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Start a new thread with <kbd>⌘N</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Next to the project, open the location menu and pick <strong>Local checkout</strong> or <strong>Worktree</strong>.</li>
      <li>Send your first message. A worktree is created now, before the agent starts.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>New threads start with the location set in <strong>Settings</strong> under <strong>General</strong>, <strong>New thread workspace</strong>: <strong>Local checkout</strong> or <strong>Isolated worktree</strong>. Tasks have their own <strong>Execution location</strong>, and schedules a <strong>Works in</strong> field. Forks, and threads an agent starts with <code>thread_start</code>, work in the same folder as the thread they came from.</p>
    <p>The message box shows the branch a thread works on, and the sidebar shows it under each thread's title.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="worktrees">
    <h2 id="worktrees">How a worktree is made</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Branch</td>
            <td><code>openorc/&lt;slug&gt;-&lt;id&gt;</code>: the thread's title when the worktree is made, in lowercase with hyphens and cut to 40 characters, then the first six characters of the thread's ID. A new thread whose first message is "Fix the login redirect" gets a branch like <code>openorc/fix-the-login-redirect-3f2a91</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Folder</td>
            <td><code>worktrees/&lt;project&gt;-&lt;id&gt;/thread-&lt;slug&gt;-&lt;id&gt;</code> inside OpenOrc's data folder, which is <code>~/Library/Application Support/OpenOrc</code> on macOS.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Starting point</td>
            <td>For a new thread, the project's default branch. OpenOrc fetches it from origin first and starts from origin's copy when the fetch works.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Before the agent starts, OpenOrc copies your <code>.env</code> and <code>.env.*</code> files into the new folder, sets up any submodules, and runs the project's setup script. Change the prefix, the copied files and the script on the project's <strong>Settings</strong> tab: press <kbd>⌘K</kbd>, choose the project, then <strong>Settings</strong>.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Project setting</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Branch prefix</strong></td>
            <td>Replaces <code>openorc</code> at the start of new branch names.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Copy these gitignored files into new worktrees</strong></td>
            <td>One file pattern per line, matched from the repository's root. Only regular files inside the repository are copied.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Setup script</strong></td>
            <td>Runs in each new worktree before the agent starts, for example <code>pnpm install --frozen-lockfile</code>. It gets <code>OPENORC_WORKSPACE_PATH</code> (the worktree), <code>OPENORC_ROOT_PATH</code> (your checkout), <code>OPENORC_WORKSPACE_NAME</code> and <code>OPENORC_PORT</code>, the first of ten ports set aside for the worktree.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Callout>If the setup script fails, the thread doesn't start, but the worktree stays. The next message uses that worktree without running the script again, so fix the problem in the worktree yourself, or delete the thread and start over.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="move">
    <h2 id="move">Move a thread</h2>
    <p>Open the thread's menu, from the three-dot button in its header or by right-clicking it in the sidebar, and choose <strong>Move to a worktree</strong> or <strong>Move to the checkout</strong>. A dialog lists every file that will move. Moving doesn't start an agent, and you can't move a thread while its agent is working.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Move to a worktree</strong> takes every uncommitted change in the checkout, including yours and other threads', into a new worktree on its own branch. The worktree starts at the checkout's current commit. Those files in the checkout then go back to its last commit.</li>
      <li><strong>Move to the checkout</strong> merges everything the worktree changed since it started, committed or not, into your checkout's files, unstaged. Then the worktree is removed. Its branch stays, with any commits made on it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If a file changed in both places in ways Git can't combine, nothing moves and the dialog names the files. A move also stops when a file's staged version differs from the file on disk; commit or unstage it first.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="merge">
    <h2 id="merge">Bring the work back</h2>
    <p><strong>Move to the checkout</strong> puts a worktree's changes in your own files, to keep editing or to commit yourself. You can also commit in the worktree, push the branch and open a pull request (see <a href={docHref("review") + "#push"}>Push and open a pull request</a>), or merge the branch into your checkout with Git, for example <code>git merge openorc/fix-the-login-redirect-3f2a91</code>. Only committed work is on the branch, so commit in the worktree before you merge.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="delete">
    <h2 id="delete">Delete a thread</h2>
    <p><strong>Delete thread</strong>, in the thread's menu, removes the conversation and its worktree:</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>OpenOrc commits the worktree's uncommitted changes to its branch, with the message "Save uncommitted work before removing the worktree".</li>
      <li>It removes the worktree folder. Ignored files in it, such as a copied <code>.env</code>, go with it.</li>
      <li>The branch stays, with all its commits.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If that commit fails, for example because Git has no user name set, nothing is deleted. Deleting never touches your checkout, and tasks the thread created stay. Archiving a thread keeps its worktree. A worktree shared with a fork or a helper thread goes only with the last thread that uses it.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="repository">
    <h2 id="repository">What OpenOrc adds to your repository</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Branches under <code>openorc/</code>, or your <strong>Branch prefix</strong>, one for each thread that used a worktree. Agent teams use branches named <code>openorc/team-…</code>.</li>
      <li>Worktree registrations under <code>.git/worktrees/</code>. The folders themselves live in OpenOrc's data folder, never inside your repository.</li>
      <li>Refs under <code>refs/openorc/</code> that keep saved file states from being cleaned up by Git: <code>checkpoints/</code> for each thread's turns, which Undo and the change cards use, and <code>snapshots/</code> for tasks that run on their own. They go when you delete the thread or task. Moves and patch exports hold a ref only while they run, and agent teams keep their own. These refs point at trees, so <code>git log --all</code> skips them and <code>git for-each-ref refs/openorc</code> lists them.</li>
      <li>Commits you make from OpenOrc, and the commit that saves a worktree's work before it is removed.</li>
      <li>Remote-tracking refs from fetching a worktree's starting branch, and the upstream that pushing sets.</li>
      <li>For a pull request a model reviews, a worktree of the pull request on no branch, and the fetched commits.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>OpenOrc installs no hooks and doesn't edit <code>.gitignore</code> or <code>.git/info/exclude</code>. It never runs <code>git stash</code> or <code>git rebase</code>, and never force-pushes.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="no-git">
    <h2 id="no-git">Projects without Git</h2>
    <p>OpenOrc reads a project folder's Git state each time it needs it, so you can run <code>git init</code> or make a first commit later without adding the project again.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">The project folder is</th>
            <th scope="col">What works</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Not a Git repository</td>
            <td>Threads work in the folder. Changes, change cards, Undo, commits and worktrees aren't available.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A repository without commits</td>
            <td>Changes, change cards, Undo and commits work. Worktrees and agent teams need a first commit.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A repository with commits</td>
            <td>Everything on this page.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>When a worktree isn't possible, the location menu says why, such as <strong>Worktrees need git and a first commit.</strong> Workspace conversations always work in a plain folder. See <a href={docHref("threads")}>Threads</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "review" }, { slug: "pull-requests" }, { slug: "storage" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
